  <!-- Headline -->
  <h2>What Actually Makes Ubigi Different</h2>
  <p>
    Ubigi isn't a travel startup that rents capacity from a phone network. It's a brand of Transatel, a French MVNO enabler that's been running cellular connectivity since the early 2000s and is now part of NTT Group, one of the largest telecom companies in the world. That's a genuinely different pedigree from most eSIM apps on this site, and it shows up in an odd place: Ubigi also sells the SIM connectivity built into connected cars from BMW, Toyota, Jeep, Maserati, Fiat, Alfa Romeo, Mini, Jaguar, and Land Rover. The travel eSIM you'd buy for a phone runs on the same underlying network infrastructure as the modem in someone's Jeep.
  </p>
  <p>
    That doesn't automatically make Ubigi the best choice for your phone. On price it's usually the more expensive option on this site: $4 for 1GB in the US is roughly double what <a href="/airhub/">Airhub</a> or <a href="/redteago/">RedteaGO</a> charge for the same gigabyte. What it does buy you is a plan structure the discount-code eSIM apps don't really offer.
  </p>

