  <!-- Real operational note instead of generic feature grid / comparison table -->
  <h2>One-Off vs Recurring: The Detail That Actually Matters Here</h2>
  <p>
    Most eSIM providers only sell one thing: a fixed data bucket that expires after a set number of days. Ubigi sells that too (its "one-off" plans), but it also sells <strong>recurring</strong> plans, and the fine print on those is worth reading before you buy, not after.
  </p>
  <p>
    A recurring <strong>monthly</strong> plan renews itself and charges your card automatically every month, and Ubigi's own help center is explicit that it can't be cancelled until you've been on it for <strong>three months minimum</strong>. A recurring <strong>annual</strong> plan is worse in one specific way: you pay the full 12 months upfront, and it <strong>cannot be cancelled or refunded during that year</strong> at all, in exchange for a fresh data allowance landing every month. Either way, whatever you don't use in a given month is gone. Ubigi doesn't roll unused data over to the next period on any plan type, one-off or recurring.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of that makes recurring plans a bad option. If you genuinely visit the same country on a regular schedule, a monthly plan can work out cheaper than buying a fresh one-off eSIM before every trip. It's just not something you want to click into by accident thinking it's a normal one-time purchase.
  </p>

